oke-width="0.71132pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 123.97 -45.23 L 146.4 -67.66"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.7071 -0.7071 0.7071 0.7071 146.4 -67.66)"><path d="M 5.41 0 C 4.75 0.16 1.83 1.09 0 2.1 L 0 -2.1 C 1.83 -1.09 4.75 -0.16 5.41 0 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.71132pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 157.48 -8.29 L 157.48 -63.07"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 157.48 -63.07)"><path d="M 5.41 0 C 4.75 0.16 1.83 1.09 0 2.1 L 0 -2.1 C 1.83 -1.09 4.75 -0.16 5.41 0 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.71132pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 126.4 -39.37 L 177.66 -39.37"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 177.66 -39.37)"><path d="M 5.41 0 C 4.75 0.16 1.83 1.09 0 2.1 L 0 -2.1 C 1.83 -1.09 4.75 -0.16 5.41 0 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#377EB8;" stroke="#377EB8" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="0.71132pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 165.32 -13.57 C 174.18 -28.92 174.18 -49.82 165.32 -65.17"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#377EB8;" fill="#377EB8" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.5 0.86603 -0.86603 -0.5 165.32 -13.57)"><path d="M 5.41 0 C 4.75 0.16 1.83 1.09 0 2.1 L 0 -2.1 C 1.83 -1.09 4.75 -0.16 5.41 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#377EB8;" fill="#377EB8" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-0.5 -0.86603 0.86603 -0.5 165.32 -65.17)"><path d="M 5.41 0 C 4.75 0.16 1.83 1.09 0 2.1 L 0 -2.1 C 1.83 -1.09 4.75 -0.16 5.41 0 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Causal diagram representing the data-generating mechanisms in a Confounded Markov Decision Process.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper, we will consistently assume the action domain <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to be discrete and finite, while the state domain <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> could be complex and continuous; the reward domain <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded in a real interval <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a demonstrator agent interacting with a CMDP <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, generating the off-policy data. For every time step <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, the environment first draws an exogenous noise <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the distribution <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathcal{U})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathcal{U})</annotation></semantics></math>; the demonstrator then performs an action <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\leftarrow f_{X}(S_{t},U_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\leftarrow f_{X}(S_{t},U_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, receives a subsequent reward <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}\leftarrow r_{t}(S_{t},X_{t},U_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}\leftarrow r_{t}(S_{t},X_{t},U_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, and moves to the next state <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t+1}\leftarrow f_{S}(S_{t},X_{t},U_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t+1}\leftarrow f_{S}(S_{t},X_{t},U_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The observed trajectories of the demonstrator (from the learner’s perspective) are summarized as the observational distribution <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(\bar{\boldsymbol{X}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{S}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑺</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bar{\boldsymbol{X}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{S}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle P(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{s}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{y}}_{1:T})=P(s_{1})\prod_{t=1}^{T}\bigg(\int_{\mathcal{U}}\boldsymbol{1}_{s_{t+1}=f_{S}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}\boldsymbol{1}_{x_{t}=f_{X}(s_{t},u_{t})}\boldsymbol{1}_{y_{h}=f_{Y}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}P(u_{t})\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{s}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{y}}_{1:T})=P(s_{1})\prod_{t=1}^{T}\bigg(\int_{\mathcal{U}}\boldsymbol{1}_{s_{t+1}=f_{S}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}\boldsymbol{1}_{x_{t}=f_{X}(s_{t},u_{t})}\boldsymbol{1}_{y_{h}=f_{Y}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}P(u_{t})\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2 Challenges Due to Unobserved Confounders ‣ Confounding Robust Deep Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the causal diagram <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>)</cite> describing the generative process of the off-policy data in CMDPs. More specifically, solid nodes represent observed variables <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t},S_{t},Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t},S_{t},Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and arrows represent the functional relationships <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{X},f_{S},f_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X},f_{S},f_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> among them. By convention, exogenous variables <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are often not explicitly shown in the graph; bi-directed arrows <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\leftarrow\rightarrow Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\leftarrow\rightarrow Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\leftarrow\rightarrow S_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\leftarrow\rightarrow S_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate the presence of an unobserved confounder (UC) <math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> affecting the action, state, and reward simultaneously.
These bi-directed arrows (highlighted in <span id="S2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#377EB8;">blue</span>) represent the unobserved confounders among action <math id="S2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, reward <math id="S2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and state <math id="S2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the off-policy data, violating the condition of NUC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>)</cite>. Such violations could lead to challenges in off-policy learning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Off-Policy Learning.</h3>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in a CMDP <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a decision rule <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(x_{t}\mid s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(x_{t}\mid s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> mapping from state to a distribution over action domain <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. An intervention <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{do}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>do</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{do}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> is an operation that replaces the behavioral policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> in CMDP <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with the policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> be the submodel induced by intervention <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{do}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>do</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{do}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math>. The interventional distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\pi}(\bar{\boldsymbol{X}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{S}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑺</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}(\bar{\boldsymbol{X}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{S}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the joint distribution over observed variables in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\pi}(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{s}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{y}}_{1:T})=P(s_{1})\prod_{t=1}^{T}\bigg(\pi(x_{t}\mid s_{t})\mathcal{T}(s_{t},x_{t},s_{t+1})\mathcal{R}(s_{t},x_{t},y_{t})\bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\pi}(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{s}}_{1:T},\bar{\boldsymbol{y}}_{1:T})=P(s_{1})\prod_{t=1}^{T}\bigg(\pi(x_{t}\mid s_{t})\mathcal{T}(s_{t},x_{t},s_{t+1})\mathcal{R}(s_{t},x_{t},y_{t})\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the transition distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and the reward distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by, for <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1,\dots,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1,\dots,H</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(s_{t},x_{t},s_{t+1})=\int_{\mathcal{U}}\boldsymbol{1}_{s_{t+1}=f_{S}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}P(u_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(s_{t},x_{t},s_{t+1})=\int_{\mathcal{U}}\boldsymbol{1}_{s_{t+1}=f_{S}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}P(u_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(s_{t},x_{t},y_{t})=\int_{\mathcal{U}}\boldsymbol{1}_{y_{t}=f_{Y}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}P(u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(s_{t},x_{t},y_{t})=\int_{\mathcal{U}}\boldsymbol{1}_{y_{t}=f_{Y}(s_{t},x_{t},u_{t})}P(u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For convenience, we write the reward function <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(s,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(s,x)</annotation></semantics></math> as the expected value <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{y}y\mathcal{R}(s,x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{y}y\mathcal{R}(s,x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix a discounted factor <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. A common objective for an agent is to optimize its cumulative return <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{i}Y_{t+i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{i}Y_{t+i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the optimal action-value function <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{*}(s,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{*}(s,x)</annotation></semantics></math> as the maximum expected return obtainable by following any policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, after seeing a state <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and taking an action <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{*}(s,x)=\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{X_{t}\leftarrow x,\pi}\left[R_{t}\mid S_{t}=s\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{*}(s,x)=\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{X_{t}\leftarrow x,\pi}\left[R_{t}\mid S_{t}=s\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. One could solve for an optimal policy by iteratively evaluating the action-value function using the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bellman Optimality Equation</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>)</cite> given by,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{*}(s,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{*}(s,x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{X_{t}\leftarrow x}\left[Y_{t}+\gamma\max_{x^{\prime}}Q_{*}(S_{t+1},x^{\prime})\mid S_{t}=s\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{X_{t}\leftarrow x}\left[Y_{t}+\gamma\max_{x^{\prime}}Q_{*}(S_{t+1},x^{\prime})\mid S_{t}=s\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In off-policy evaluation, the agent (i.e., learner) attempts to learn an optimal policy by leveraging the observed data generated by a different behavior policy <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (demonstrator). When there is no unmeasured confounder (NUC) introducing spurious correlations between action and subsequent outcomes, one could identify the parameterizations of the transition distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and reward function <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> from the observed data, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(s_{t},x_{t},s_{t+1})=P\left(s_{t+1}\mid s_{t},x_{t}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}(s_{t},x_{t},s_{t+1})=P\left(s_{t+1}\mid s_{t},x_{t}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(s_{t},x_{t},y_{t})=P\left(y_{t}\mid s_{t},x_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(s_{t},x_{t},y_{t})=P\left(y_{t}\mid s_{t},x_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">When the above identification formula hold, several off-policy algorithms have been proposed to estimate the effect of candidate policies from finite observations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>, <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>, <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>)</cite>. Together with the computational framework of deep learning, these methods could be further extended to complex domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>)</cite>. However, NUC could be fragile in practice and does not necessarily hold due to some violations in the generative process. In these situations, applying standard off-policy methods may fail to converge to an optimal policy, despite using powerful deep learning models. The following example illustrates such challenges in a classic Atari game.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span><span id="Thmexample1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Confounded Pong)<span id="Thmexample1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the Pong game in the classic Atari suite. As for the behavior policy, we use a pre-trained high-performing actor-critic agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>)</cite> with residual blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>)</cite> and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>)</cite>) layers. <a href="#S2.F2.sf1" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ Off-Policy Learning. ‣ 2 Challenges Due to Unobserved Confounders ‣ Confounding Robust Deep Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> shows a saliency map visualizing the learned policy. Simulation results show that this policy is optimal. For example, this agent can deliver a “kill shot” by directing the ball to a location that the opponent is unlikely to intercept given its current location. We use this agent as the demonstrator in the off-policy learning task.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider an alternative agent that learns to play Pong by observing the demonstrator’s gameplay trajectories. This learning agent has a simpler neural network architecture and an impaired sensory capability: it can only observe movements in its nearby surroundings. <a href="#S2.F2.sf2" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ Off-Policy Learning. ‣ 2 Challenges Due to Unobserved Confounders ‣ Confounding Robust Deep Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> shows the learner’s visual input; the board’s left-hand side and the upper side, including the opponent’s position and score, is now masked. In this case, the opponent’s position becomes an unobserved confounder, introducing spurious correlations between the demonstrator’s action and observed outcome. For example, the behavioral policy tends to hit the ball toward the center only when the opponent is positioned at either corner and unable to return it. As a result, center shots appear more effective than they truly are, due to confounding resulted from the masked opponent’s position.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To validate whether the standard deep RL algorithms are robust to confounding biases, we train two DQN learners on masked trajectories from the demonstrator: one is the standard convolutional neural network based DQN (Nature DQN, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>)</cite>), the second one is an LSTM-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>)</cite>. We also include a standard DQN that learns directly in the masked Pong game without confounded demonstrations as a baseline. The simulation results, shown in <a href="#S2.F2.sf4" title="In Fig. 2 ‣ Off-Policy Learning. ‣ 2 Challenges Due to Unobserved Confounders ‣ Confounding Robust Deep Reinforcement Learning: A Causal Approach" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(d)</span></a>, indicate that none of those DQN variants is able to converge to an effective policy. Learning directly with impaired observation in Atari is challenging, while incorporating confounded demonstrations directly also does not enhance the convergence of DQN agents; instead, it negatively impacts their learning performance. <math id="Thmexample1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hfill\blacksquare" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">■</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hfill\blacksquare</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
